## R&D Handoff Acceptance

| Check | Acceptance Standard |
|---|---|
| Scope can be planned | P0/P1/Later/Out of Scope are explicit enough for later technical planning. |
| User flow can be implemented | Main flow, branch flow, and exit/fallback paths are described without hidden steps. |
| State coverage is complete | Default, empty, error, success, permission/login, and reverse/exit states are defined where applicable. |
| Data requirements are usable | Product entities, mock data, and visualization units are clear enough to design technical data models later. |
| Acceptance can be tested | Each P0 behavior has observable product acceptance criteria. |

## Customer / Business Acceptance

| Check | Acceptance Standard |
|---|---|
| Value is understandable | Customer/business readers can explain who the product serves and what value it creates. |
| Scope is understandable | Customer/business readers can distinguish MVP scope from later ideas. |
| Experience is understandable | Customer/business readers can follow the main user path and expected result. |

## Preview UX Acceptance

| Check | Acceptance Standard |
|---|---|
| First-screen value | A target user can understand the product value from the first screen without reading PRD text. |
| Primary CTA clarity | The main action is visible and matches the minimum value loop. |
| Main path clarity | The primary user path can be completed in a small number of clear steps. |
| Feedback clarity | Success, empty, error, and blocked states provide clear next steps. |
| PRD consistency | Preview pages, states, and mock data match `MVP.md`, `user-flow.md`, and `data-and-states.md`. |

